{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_012NfSJwiQn4Fx1ZxN3QFYw7","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t\\section{Related Work}\n2\t\n3\t\\paragraph{Training-Free Activation Sparsity.}\n4\tTEAL~\\cite{liu2025teal} applies global top-$k$ selection by activation magnitude and achieves 40--50\\% sparsity with no training cost.\n5\tCATS~\\cite{lee2024cats} adapts per-layer thresholds for SiLU/SwiGLU activations; R-Sparse~\\cite{zhang2025rsparse} predicts sparsity from rank-aware SVD projections; DIP~\\cite{federici2025dip} combines dynamic input pruning with cache-aware masking; SparseInfer~\\cite{shin2025sparseinfer} predicts activation sparsity patterns from attention outputs without additional training.\n6\tAll training-free methods determine masks from fixed statistics or input-independent criteria, while our predictor generates \\emph{input-dependent} masks trained to minimize output-distribution divergence.\n7\t\n8\t\\paragraph{Predictor-Based Contextual Sparsity.}\n9\tDejaVu~\\cite{liu2023dejavu} introduced learned sparsity predictors trained via BCE to classify each neuron independently.\n10\tShadowLLM~\\cite{akhauri2024shadowllm} uses attention-based routing with per-layer MSE supervision for intermediate coupling within each layer but without end-to-end gradient signal.\n11\tFastForward~\\cite{gautam2026fastforward} adds a 67.2M-parameter compensation network using BCE for mask prediction, adding inference latency.\n12\tSVD-CSP~\\cite{serbin2026svdcsp} compresses sparsity predictors via SVD decomposition for reduced predictor overhead.\n13\tPowerInfer~\\cite{song2024powerinfer} exploits power-law activation frequencies for GPU--CPU scheduling.\n14\tMaskLLM~\\cite{fang2024maskllm} also employs learnable masks with Gumbel relaxation and frozen weights, but targets N:M semi-structured \\emph{weight} sparsity (2:4 patterns) via per-group categorical selection (Gumbel-Softmax), producing fixed masks independent of input tokens.\n15\tBoth share the principle of frozen weights + learnable masks + KL training, but solve distinct optimization problems: MaskLLM finds optimal static N:M patterns within fixed groups, while we predict dynamic per-token masks under a global budget constraint.\n16\tThe structural mismatch we characterize (per-neuron gradient decomposition vs.\\ global top-$k$) does not arise in MaskLLM's per-group categorical setting, where N:M patterns eliminate cross-group budget competition.\n17\tAll prior predictor-based methods use decomposed (BCE) or intermediate (MSE) objectives where each neuron's gradient is independent of other mask states.\n18\tOur contribution is primarily empirical: we observe that a coupled KL objective propagates gradients through all downstream layers and quantify when this matters for global allocation quality.\n19\t\n20\t\\paragraph{Static Bias Correction.}\n21\tSPON~\\cite{xu2026spon} learns input-independent bias vectors via closed-form KL minimization per layer, absorbed into weights at deployment.\n22\tOur method is fundamentally different: a per-token contextual predictor trained end-to-end across all layers, producing input-dependent masks.\n23\tSPON solves a static per-layer problem; we solve a global dynamic prediction problem. The two are weakly correlated (cosine similarity 0.196, $R^2$=0.038; \\S\\ref{sec:experiments}).\n24\t\n25\t\\paragraph{Activation Function Engineering.}\n26\tReLU Strikes Back~\\cite{mirzadeh2024relu} replaces SiLU with ReLU for $>$90\\% natural sparsity; TurboSparse~\\cite{song2024turbosparse} and ProSparse~\\cite{song2025prosparse} engineer extreme sparsity through activation modifications; the Sparsing Law~\\cite{luo2024sparsinglaw} formalizes how activation density increases with training compute.\n27\tMiniTron~\\cite{muralidharan2024minitron} combines structured pruning with distillation but modifies architecture permanently.\n28\tAll require retraining or weight modification; our method is a plug-in predictor on \\emph{unmodified} SwiGLU models.\n29\t\n30\t\\paragraph{Weight Pruning, Distillation, and Routing.}\n31\tWanda~\\cite{sun2024wanda} and SparseGPT~\\cite{frantar2023sparsegpt} produce static weight masks; Switch Transformers~\\cite{fedus2022switch} and Mixture-of-Depths~\\cite{raposo2024mod} achieve conditional computation through learned routing.\n32\tKL-based distillation~\\cite{kim2016sequencekd,sanh2019distilbert,gu2024minillm,agarwal2024gkd} trains compact students; SparseLoRA~\\cite{khaki2025sparselora} exploits contextual sparsity for fine-tuning.\n33\tWe apply the KL principle to train a sparsity predictor on unmodified dense models, using Gumbel-Sigmoid sampling~\\cite{maddison2017concrete,louizos2018l0} for differentiable discrete mask optimization.\n34\t\n35\t\\paragraph{MoE Routing and Gated Sparsity.}\n36\tExpert Choice routing~\\cite{zhou2022expertchoice} lets each expert select its top-$k$ tokens under a fixed capacity budget, a global allocation perspective that parallels TEAL's neuron-level global top-$k$.\n37\tD2D-MoE~\\cite{szatkowski2024d2dmoe} converts dense SwiGLU layers into dynamic-$k$ MoE by exploiting the same activation sparsity we target, but restructures the network into discrete experts rather than predicting masks on the original architecture.\n38\tGated architectures beyond SwiGLU (e.g., Griffin's RG-LRU~\\cite{de2024griffin}) produce analogous structured sparsity through their gating mechanisms, suggesting that output-distribution prediction may generalize beyond standard Transformer FFNs.\n39\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01QSVrioRM1nc2c8wAncaLmD","session_id":"20b0e106-df0c-43c4-9dfa-fa82e00c09d1","uuid":"2b727a6a-4444-4e61-9a8b-2d9a304a94d2","timestamp":"2026-05-18T18:31:05.719Z"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_019rekv63bcNEpyWwXf9ffTE","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_018N6LYasBXquWk11d4UTTdE","name":"Agent","input":{"description":"R2: Novelty + Related Work review","prompt":"You are Reviewer 2 (R2) for a top ML venue peer review. Your focus is **novelty and related work**. Score on a 6-point scale: 1=Strong Reject, 2=Reject, 3=Weak Reject, 4=Weak Accept, 5=Accept, 6=Strong Accept. Acceptance rate ~25%.\n\n## Paper summary\n\n**Title**: \"What Does KL Distillation Learn for SwiGLU Sparsity Prediction? Mask Pattern Discovery Beyond Magnitude Oracles\"\n\n**Core idea**: Replace BCE loss (used by FastForward) with KL-divergence distillation for training lightweight sparsity predictors in SwiGLU LLMs. KL-trained masks diverge 53% from magnitude-based oracles, revealing a fundamentally different neuron importance landscape. A causal ablation shows target quality (the mask patterns themselves) accounts for >95% of KL's advantage, with gradient coupling only a marginal contributor. This eliminates the need for compensation networks (67.2M params). Three sparsity regimes identified.\n\n**Models**: Llama-3.1-8B, Mistral-7B-v0.3\n**Method (Section 3)**:\n- §3.1: Problem setup — train lightweight predictor p_θ per-token, binary mask m ∈ {0,1}^d_ff, two-layer MLP with Gumbel-sigmoid\n- §3.2: KL distillation loss: L_KL = D_KL(P_dense || P_sparse(m_θ)), Lagrangian sparsity constraint\n- §3.3: Gradient analysis — BCE gradient is factorized (depends only on neuron j), KL gradient couples all neurons via output distribution\n- §3.4: Comparison with BCE+Compensation (FastForward's approach)\n\n**Related Work (Section 2)** covers three areas:\n1. **Training-free activation sparsity**: TEAL (magnitude + global allocation), WINA (weight-informed), R-Sparse (rank-aware). Notes these degrade rapidly above 30%.\n2. **Learned sparsity predictors**: FastForward (BCE + compensation MLP), PowerInfer (profiled activation frequencies). Claims to replace both BCE loss and magnitude oracle.\n3. **Static correction via distillation**: SPON (per-layer bias vectors via KL), ShadowLLM (per-layer MSE). Both input-independent. Paper shows SPON near-orthogonal to KL (cosine 0.196).\n\n**Key claims**:\n1. KL-derived mask patterns (not gradient coupling) are the dominant driver (>95%) of quality advantage\n2. 53% mask divergence from magnitude oracles is a genuine empirical finding about neuron importance\n3. Compensation parameters become redundant under KL training\n4. Three sparsity regimes with different allocation preferences\n\n**Results highlights**:\n- KL-only PPL 9.19 vs BCE+comp 20.80 at 50% (Table 1)\n- KL 30% uniform PPL 8.82 vs TEAL 10.97 (Table 2)\n- C1 ablation: BCE+KL-targets within 0.22 PPL of full KL (Table 3)\n- Cross-arch: KL/TEAL ratio consistent on Mistral (1.24×/2.12×/4.63×) vs Llama (1.24×/1.98×/5.59×)\n- No wall-clock speedup (0.94× at 30%, needs sparse kernels)\n- SPON recovery only 7.3%, cosine similarity 0.196\n\n**Limitations acknowledged**: 7-8B scale only (14B MMLU disconnect), no wall-clock speedup, long-context degradation (72%→41% 4K→32K), single-seed noise at 35%\n\n## Your review task\n\nEvaluate the paper on these axes:\n1. **Novelty**: Is the contribution genuinely new? Is \"use KL instead of BCE\" a sufficient contribution? How novel is the causal ablation finding? Is 53% mask divergence a surprising finding or expected?\n2. **Related work coverage**: Are key baselines and prior work adequately cited? Any missing references? Is positioning accurate?\n3. **Significance**: Does this advance the field meaningfully? Would practitioners adopt this?\n4. **Framing and narrative**: Is the paper positioned correctly? Does the story from abstract to conclusion hold together? Are claims calibrated?\n5. **Broader context**: Where does this sit in the activation sparsity literature? Is the contribution incremental or substantive?\n\nProvide:\n- A structured review with Strengths (S1, S2, ...) and Weaknesses (W1, W2, ...)\n- Questions for authors (Q1, Q2, ...)\n- Minor issues\n- Overall score and confidence (1-5)\n- 2-3 sentence summary justification\n\nBe rigorous. Consider whether the novelty meets the bar for a top venue (~25% acceptance). Think about: is \"changing the loss function from BCE to KL\" enough, or does the deeper analysis (causal ablation, mask divergence finding, regime analysis) elevate the contribution?","model":"opus"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":3,"cache_creation_input_tokens":31164,"cache_read_input_tokens":25058,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":31164},"output_tokens":16,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"20b0e106-df0c-43c4-9dfa-fa82e00c09d1","uuid":"243c65e9-0ec1-43bf-a4d8-c1ec1a18f2a3"}
{"type":"system","subtype":"task_started","task_id":"a95374fe78c4d9239","tool_use_id":"toolu_018N6LYasBXquWk11d4UTTdE","description":"R2: Novelty + Related Work review","task_type":"local_agent","prompt":"You are Reviewer 2 (R2) for a top ML venue peer review. Your focus is **novelty and related work**. Score on a 6-point scale: 1=Strong Reject, 2=Reject, 3=Weak Reject, 4=Weak Accept, 5=Accept, 6=Strong Accept. Acceptance rate ~25%.\n\n## Paper summary\n\n**Title**: \"What Does KL Distillation Learn for SwiGLU Sparsity Prediction? Mask Pattern Discovery Beyond Magnitude Oracles\"\n\n**Core idea**: Replace BCE loss (used by FastForward) with KL-divergence distillation for training lightweight sparsity predictors in SwiGLU LLMs. KL-trained masks diverge 53% from magnitude-based oracles, revealing a fundamentally different neuron importance landscape. A causal ablation shows target quality (the mask patterns themselves) accounts for >95% of KL's advantage, with gradient coupling only a marginal contributor. This eliminates the need for compensation networks (67.2M params). Three sparsity regimes identified.\n\n**Models**: Llama-3.1-8B, Mistral-7B-v0.3\n**Method (Section 3)**:\n- §3.1: Problem setup — train lightweight predictor p_θ per-token, binary mask m ∈ {0,1}^d_ff, two-layer MLP with Gumbel-sigmoid\n- §3.2: KL distillation loss: L_KL = D_KL(P_dense || P_sparse(m_θ)), Lagrangian sparsity constraint\n- §3.3: Gradient analysis — BCE gradient is factorized (depends only on neuron j), KL gradient couples all neurons via output distribution\n- §3.4: Comparison with BCE+Compensation (FastForward's approach)\n\n**Related Work (Section 2)** covers three areas:\n1. **Training-free activation sparsity**: TEAL (magnitude + global allocation), WINA (weight-informed), R-Sparse (rank-aware). Notes these degrade rapidly above 30%.\n2. **Learned sparsity predictors**: FastForward (BCE + compensation MLP), PowerInfer (profiled activation frequencies). Claims to replace both BCE loss and magnitude oracle.\n3. **Static correction via distillation**: SPON (per-layer bias vectors via KL), ShadowLLM (per-layer MSE). Both input-independent. Paper shows SPON near-orthogonal to KL (cosine 0.196).\n\n**Key claims**:\n1. KL-derived mask patterns (not gradient coupling) are the dominant driver (>95%) of quality advantage\n2. 53% mask divergence from magnitude oracles is a genuine empirical finding about neuron importance\n3. Compensation parameters become redundant under KL training\n4. Three sparsity regimes with different allocation preferences\n\n**Results highlights**:\n- KL-only PPL 9.19 vs BCE+comp 20.80 at 50% (Table 1)\n- KL 30% uniform PPL 8.82 vs TEAL 10.97 (Table 2)\n- C1 ablation: BCE+KL-targets within 0.22 PPL of full KL (Table 3)\n- Cross-arch: KL/TEAL ratio consistent on Mistral (1.24×/2.12×/4.63×) vs Llama (1.24×/1.98×/5.59×)\n- No wall-clock speedup (0.94× at 30%, needs sparse kernels)\n- SPON recovery only 7.3%, cosine similarity 0.196\n\n**Limitations acknowledged**: 7-8B scale only (14B MMLU disconnect), no wall-clock speedup, long-context degradation (72%→41% 4K→32K), single-seed noise at 35%\n\n## Your review task\n\nEvaluate the paper on these axes:\n1. **Novelty**: Is the contribution genuinely new? Is \"use KL instead of BCE\" a sufficient contribution? How novel is the causal ablation finding? Is 53% mask divergence a surprising finding or expected?\n2. **Related work coverage**: Are key baselines and prior work adequately cited? Any missing references? Is positioning accurate?\n3. **Significance**: Does this advance the field meaningfully? Would practitioners adopt this?\n4. **Framing and narrative**: Is the paper positioned correctly? Does the story from abstract to conclusion hold together? Are claims calibrated?\n5. **Broader context**: Where does this sit in the activation sparsity literature? Is the contribution incremental or substantive?\n\nProvide:\n- A structured review with Strengths (S1, S2, ...) and Weaknesses (W1, W2, ...)\n- Questions for authors (Q1, Q2, ...)\n- Minor issues\n- Overall score and confidence (1-5)\n- 2-3 sentence summary justification\n\nBe rigorous. Consider whether the novelty meets the bar for a top venue (~25% acceptance). Think about: is \"changing the loss function from BCE to KL\" enough, or does the deeper analysis (causal ablation, mask divergence finding, regime analysis) elevate the contribution?","uuid":"055d0a70-86b8-4599-bbc7-cdcead668d43","session_id":"20b0e106-df0c-43c4-9dfa-fa82e00c09d1"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"text","text":"You are Reviewer 2 (R2) for a top ML venue peer review. Your focus is **novelty and related work**. Score on a 6-point scale: 1=Strong Reject, 2=Reject, 3=Weak Reject, 4=Weak Accept, 5=Accept, 6=Strong Accept. Acceptance rate ~25%.\n\n## Paper summary\n\n**Title**: \"What Does KL Distillation Learn for SwiGLU Sparsity Prediction? Mask Pattern Discovery Beyond Magnitude Oracles\"\n\n**Core idea**: Replace BCE loss (used by FastForward) with KL-divergence distillation for training lightweight sparsity predictors in SwiGLU LLMs. KL-trained masks diverge 53% from magnitude-based oracles, revealing a fundamentally different neuron importance landscape. A causal ablation shows target quality (the mask patterns themselves) accounts for >95% of KL's advantage, with gradient coupling only a marginal contributor. This eliminates the need for compensation networks (67.2M params). Three sparsity regimes identified.\n\n**Models**: Llama-3.1-8B, Mistral-7B-v0.3\n**Method (Section 3)**:\n- §3.1: Problem setup — train lightweight predictor p_θ per-token, binary mask m ∈ {0,1}^d_ff, two-layer MLP with Gumbel-sigmoid\n- §3.2: KL distillation loss: L_KL = D_KL(P_dense || P_sparse(m_θ)), Lagrangian sparsity constraint\n- §3.3: Gradient analysis — BCE gradient is factorized (depends only on neuron j), KL gradient couples all neurons via output distribution\n- §3.4: Comparison with BCE+Compensation (FastForward's approach)\n\n**Related Work (Section 2)** covers three areas:\n1. **Training-free activation sparsity**: TEAL (magnitude + global allocation), WINA (weight-informed), R-Sparse (rank-aware). Notes these degrade rapidly above 30%.\n2. **Learned sparsity predictors**: FastForward (BCE + compensation MLP), PowerInfer (profiled activation frequencies). Claims to replace both BCE loss and magnitude oracle.\n3. **Static correction via distillation**: SPON (per-layer bias vectors via KL), ShadowLLM (per-layer MSE). Both input-independent. Paper shows SPON near-orthogonal to KL (cosine 0.196).\n\n**Key claims**:\n1. KL-derived mask patterns (not gradient coupling) are the dominant driver (>95%) of quality advantage\n2. 53% mask divergence from magnitude oracles is a genuine empirical finding about neuron importance\n3. Compensation parameters become redundant under KL training\n4. Three sparsity regimes with different allocation preferences\n\n**Results highlights**:\n- KL-only PPL 9.19 vs BCE+comp 20.80 at 50% (Table 1)\n- KL 30% uniform PPL 8.82 vs TEAL 10.97 (Table 2)\n- C1 ablation: BCE+KL-targets within 0.22 PPL of full KL (Table 3)\n- Cross-arch: KL/TEAL ratio consistent on Mistral (1.24×/2.12×/4.63×) vs Llama (1.24×/1.98×/5.59×)\n- No wall-clock speedup (0.94× at 30%, needs sparse kernels)\n- SPON recovery only 7.3%, cosine similarity 0.196\n\n**Limitations acknowledged**: 7-8B scale only (14B MMLU disconnect), no wall-clock speedup, long-context degradation (72%→41% 4K→32K), single-seed noise at 35%\n\n## Your review task\n\nEvaluate the paper on these axes:\n1. **Novelty**: Is the contribution genuinely new? Is \"use KL instead of BCE\" a sufficient contribution? How novel is the causal ablation finding? Is 53% mask divergence a surprising finding or expected?\n2. **Related work coverage**: Are key baselines and prior work adequately cited? Any missing references? Is positioning accurate?\n3. **Significance**: Does this advance the field meaningfully? Would practitioners adopt this?\n4. **Framing and narrative**: Is the paper positioned correctly? Does the story from abstract to conclusion hold together? Are claims calibrated?\n5. **Broader context**: Where does this sit in the activation sparsity literature? Is the contribution incremental or substantive?\n\nProvide:\n- A structured review with Strengths (S1, S2, ...) and Weaknesses (W1, W2, ...)\n- Questions for authors (Q1, Q2, ...)\n- Minor issues\n- Overall score and confidence (1-5)\n- 2-3 sentence summary justification\n\nBe rigorous. Consider whether the novelty meets the bar for a top venue (~25% acceptance). Think about: is \"changing the loss function from BCE to KL\" enough, or does the deeper analysis (causal ablation, mask divergence finding, regime analysis) elevate the contribution?"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_018N6LYasBXquWk11d4UTTdE","session_id":"20b0e106-df0c-43c4-9dfa-fa82e00c09d1","uuid":"5a63b214-5c6c-4f98-ab97-951b7449ade4","timestamp":"2026-05-18T18:31:06.733Z"}
