{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01Fhm7CcJBxvMy46em1BvMFP","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# Verify KL-derived oracle masks: load trained KL predictor, generate binary masks on\n2\t# calibration data, report per-layer sparsity and agreement with magnitude-based masks.\n3\t# Full masks are generated on-the-fly during training (too large to pre-store on disk).\n4\t\n5\timport argparse\n6\timport importlib.util\n7\timport os\n8\timport sys\n9\t\n10\timport torch\n11\tfrom transformers import AutoModelForCausalLM, AutoTokenizer\n12\t\n13\tsys.path.insert(0, os.path.dirname(os.path.abspath(__file__)))\n14\tfrom data_utils import get_calibration_loader\n15\tfrom predictor import PredictorWrapper\n16\t\n17\t\n18\tdef _best_attn_impl():\n19\t    if importlib.util.find_spec(\"flash_attn\"):\n20\t        return \"flash_attention_2\"\n21\t    return \"sdpa\"\n22\t\n23\t\n24\tdef main():\n25\t    p = argparse.ArgumentParser(description=\"Generate and verify KL oracle masks\")\n26\t    p.add_argument(\"--model_name_or_path\", type=str,\n27\t                   default=\"/root/autodl-tmp/models/llama-3.1-8b\")\n28\t    p.add_argument(\"--kl_checkpoint\", type=str, required=True,\n29\t                   help=\"Path to trained KL predictor checkpoint\")\n30\t    p.add_argument(\"--gpu\", type=int, default=0)\n31\t    p.add_argument(\"--batch_size\", type=int, default=4)\n32\t    p.add_argument(\"--seq_len\", type=int, default=2048)\n33\t    p.add_argument(\"--seed\", type=int, default=42)\n34\t    p.add_argument(\"--num_verify_batches\", type=int, default=5)\n35\t    p.add_argument(\"--sparsity_target\", type=float, default=0.5,\n36\t                   help=\"Expected sparsity for magnitude-based comparison\")\n37\t    p.add_argument(\"--output_path\", type=str, default=None,\n38\t                   help=\"Save sample masks to this path (optional)\")\n39\t    p.add_argument(\"--bottleneck_dim\", type=int, default=128)\n40\t    args = p.parse_args()\n41\t\n42\t    device = torch.device(f\"cuda:{args.gpu}\")\n43\t\n44\t    print(f\"Loading model from {args.model_name_or_path} ...\")\n45\t    tokenizer = AutoTokenizer.from_pretrained(args.model_name_or_path)\n46\t    if tokenizer.pad_token is None:\n47\t        tokenizer.pad_token = tokenizer.eos_token\n48\t    model = AutoModelForCausalLM.from_pretrained(\n49\t        args.model_name_or_path,\n50\t        torch_dtype=torch.bfloat16,\n51\t        device_map={\"\": device},\n52\t        attn_implementation=_best_attn_impl(),\n53\t    )\n54\t    model.eval()\n55\t\n56\t    wrapper = PredictorWrapper(model, bottleneck_size=args.bottleneck_dim)\n57\t\n58\t    print(f\"Loading KL predictor from {args.kl_checkpoint} ...\")\n59\t    ckpt = torch.load(args.kl_checkpoint, map_location=device, weights_only=True)\n60\t    wrapper.predictors.load_state_dict(ckpt[\"predictors\"])\n61\t    wrapper.predictors.to(device=device, dtype=torch.bfloat16)\n62\t    wrapper.predictors.eval()\n63\t\n64\t    print(f\"Loading calibration data (wikitext-103, seed={args.seed}) ...\")\n65\t    dataloader = get_calibration_loader(tokenizer, args.batch_size, args.seq_len, args.seed)\n66\t    data_iter = iter(dataloader)\n67\t\n68\t    num_layers = len(wrapper.predictors)\n69\t    layer_density_sum = [0.0] * num_layers\n70\t    layer_agreement_sum = [0.0] * num_layers\n71\t    layer_count = [0] * num_layers\n72\t\n73\t    print(f\"\\nVerifying {args.num_verify_batches} batches ...\")\n74\t    for batch_idx in range(args.num_verify_batches):\n75\t        batch = next(data_iter)\n76\t        input_ids = batch[\"input_ids\"].to(device)\n77\t\n78\t        wrapper.forward_dense(input_ids, capture_intermediates=True)\n79\t        layer_inputs = wrapper.get_layer_inputs()\n80\t        intermediates = wrapper.get_intermediates()\n81\t\n82\t        for layer_idx in range(num_layers):\n83\t            if layer_idx not in layer_inputs:\n84\t                continue\n85\t            inp = layer_inputs[layer_idx]\n86\t            with torch.no_grad():\n87\t                kl_logits = wrapper.predictors[layer_idx](inp)\n88\t            kl_mask = (kl_logits > 0).float()\n89\t\n90\t            density = kl_mask.mean().item()\n91\t            layer_density_sum[layer_idx] += density\n92\t            layer_count[layer_idx] += 1\n93\t\n94\t            # Magnitude-based mask for comparison\n95\t            dense_act = intermediates[layer_idx]\n96\t            k = int(dense_act.shape[-1] * (1 - args.sparsity_target))\n97\t            abs_vals = dense_act.abs()\n98\t            topk_vals, _ = abs_vals.topk(k, dim=-1)\n99\t            threshold = topk_vals[..., -1:]\n100\t            mag_mask = (abs_vals >= threshold).float()\n101\t\n102\t            agreement = ((kl_mask == mag_mask).float().mean().item())\n103\t            layer_agreement_sum[layer_idx] += agreement\n104\t\n105\t            if batch_idx == 0 and layer_idx % 8 == 0:\n106\t                print(f\"  Layer {layer_idx:2d}: KL density={density:.3f}, \"\n107\t                      f\"KL-vs-magnitude agreement={agreement:.3f}\")\n108\t\n109\t        if batch_idx == 0:\n110\t            print(f\"  (showing every 8th layer for batch 0)\")\n111\t\n112\t    print(f\"\\n=== Per-layer stats (mean over {args.num_verify_batches} batches) ===\")\n113\t    for layer_idx in range(num_layers):\n114\t        if layer_count[layer_idx] > 0:\n115\t            avg_density = layer_density_sum[layer_idx] / layer_count[layer_idx]\n116\t            avg_agreement = layer_agreement_sum[layer_idx] / layer_count[layer_idx]\n117\t            print(f\"  Layer {layer_idx:2d}: density={avg_density:.3f} \"\n118\t                  f\"(sparsity={1-avg_density:.3f})  agreement={avg_agreement:.3f}\")\n119\t\n120\t    total_density = sum(layer_density_sum) / max(sum(layer_count), 1)\n121\t    total_agreement = sum(layer_agreement_sum) / max(sum(layer_count), 1)\n122\t    print(f\"\\n  Global: density={total_density:.3f} (sparsity={1-total_density:.3f})  \"\n123\t          f\"agreement={total_agreement:.3f}\")\n124\t\n125\t    if args.output_path:\n126\t        # Save one batch of masks as sample\n127\t        batch = next(data_iter)\n128\t        input_ids = batch[\"input_ids\"].to(device)\n129\t        wrapper.forward_dense(input_ids, capture_intermediates=True)\n130\t        layer_inputs = wrapper.get_layer_inputs()\n131\t        sample = {}\n132\t        for layer_idx in range(num_layers):\n133\t            if layer_idx not in layer_inputs:\n134\t                continue\n135\t            with torch.no_grad():\n136\t                kl_logits = wrapper.predictors[layer_idx](layer_inputs[layer_idx])\n137\t            sample[layer_idx] = (kl_logits > 0).cpu()\n138\t        torch.save(sample, args.output_path)\n139\t        print(f\"\\nSaved sample masks ({len(sample)} layers) -> {args.output_path}\")\n140\t\n141\t    print(\"\\nVerification complete.\")\n142\t\n143\t\n144\tif __name__ == \"__main__\":\n145\t    main()\n146\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01QSVrioRM1nc2c8wAncaLmD","session_id":"20b0e106-df0c-43c4-9dfa-fa82e00c09d1","uuid":"0213810d-14a4-48ca-9e1d-f77f59d443df","timestamp":"2026-05-18T18:31:18.546Z"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01VNygNrmUakVSoVZZYbovp6","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t\\section{Related Work}\n2\t\n3\t\\paragraph{Training-Free Activation Sparsity.}\n4\tTEAL~\\cite{liu2025teal} applies global top-$k$ selection by activation magnitude and achieves 40--50\\% sparsity with no training cost.\n5\tCATS~\\cite{lee2024cats} adapts per-layer thresholds for SiLU/SwiGLU activations; R-Sparse~\\cite{zhang2025rsparse} predicts sparsity from rank-aware SVD projections; DIP~\\cite{federici2025dip} combines dynamic input pruning with cache-aware masking; SparseInfer~\\cite{shin2025sparseinfer} predicts activation sparsity patterns from attention outputs without additional training.\n6\tAll training-free methods determine masks from fixed statistics or input-independent criteria, while our predictor generates \\emph{input-dependent} masks trained to minimize output-distribution divergence.\n7\t\n8\t\\paragraph{Predictor-Based Contextual Sparsity.}\n9\tDejaVu~\\cite{liu2023dejavu} introduced learned sparsity predictors trained via BCE to classify each neuron independently.\n10\tShadowLLM~\\cite{akhauri2024shadowllm} uses attention-based routing with per-layer MSE supervision for intermediate coupling within each layer but without end-to-end gradient signal.\n11\tFastForward~\\cite{gautam2026fastforward} adds a 67.2M-parameter compensation network using BCE for mask prediction, adding inference latency.\n12\tSVD-CSP~\\cite{serbin2026svdcsp} compresses sparsity predictors via SVD decomposition for reduced predictor overhead.\n13\tPowerInfer~\\cite{song2024powerinfer} exploits power-law activation frequencies for GPU--CPU scheduling.\n14\tMaskLLM~\\cite{fang2024maskllm} also employs learnable masks with Gumbel relaxation and frozen weights, but targets N:M semi-structured \\emph{weight} sparsity (2:4 patterns) via per-group categorical selection (Gumbel-Softmax), producing fixed masks independent of input tokens.\n15\tBoth share the principle of frozen weights + learnable masks + KL training, but solve distinct optimization problems: MaskLLM finds optimal static N:M patterns within fixed groups, while we predict dynamic per-token masks under a global budget constraint.\n16\tThe structural mismatch we characterize (per-neuron gradient decomposition vs.\\ global top-$k$) does not arise in MaskLLM's per-group categorical setting, where N:M patterns eliminate cross-group budget competition.\n17\tAll prior predictor-based methods use decomposed (BCE) or intermediate (MSE) objectives where each neuron's gradient is independent of other mask states.\n18\tOur contribution is primarily empirical: we observe that a coupled KL objective propagates gradients through all downstream layers and quantify when this matters for global allocation quality.\n19\t\n20\t\\paragraph{Static Bias Correction.}\n21\tSPON~\\cite{xu2026spon} learns input-independent bias vectors via closed-form KL minimization per layer, absorbed into weights at deployment.\n22\tOur method is fundamentally different: a per-token contextual predictor trained end-to-end across all layers, producing input-dependent masks.\n23\tSPON solves a static per-layer problem; we solve a global dynamic prediction problem. The two are weakly correlated (cosine similarity 0.196, $R^2$=0.038; \\S\\ref{sec:experiments}).\n24\t\n25\t\\paragraph{Activation Function Engineering.}\n26\tReLU Strikes Back~\\cite{mirzadeh2024relu} replaces SiLU with ReLU for $>$90\\% natural sparsity; TurboSparse~\\cite{song2024turbosparse} and ProSparse~\\cite{song2025prosparse} engineer extreme sparsity through activation modifications; the Sparsing Law~\\cite{luo2024sparsinglaw} formalizes how activation density increases with training compute.\n27\tMiniTron~\\cite{muralidharan2024minitron} combines structured pruning with distillation but modifies architecture permanently.\n28\tAll require retraining or weight modification; our method is a plug-in predictor on \\emph{unmodified} SwiGLU models.\n29\t\n30\t\\paragraph{Weight Pruning, Distillation, and Routing.}\n31\tWanda~\\cite{sun2024wanda} and SparseGPT~\\cite{frantar2023sparsegpt} produce static weight masks; Switch Transformers~\\cite{fedus2022switch} and Mixture-of-Depths~\\cite{raposo2024mod} achieve conditional computation through learned routing.\n32\tKL-based distillation~\\cite{kim2016sequencekd,sanh2019distilbert,gu2024minillm,agarwal2024gkd} trains compact students; SparseLoRA~\\cite{khaki2025sparselora} exploits contextual sparsity for fine-tuning.\n33\tWe apply the KL principle to train a sparsity predictor on unmodified dense models, using Gumbel-Sigmoid sampling~\\cite{maddison2017concrete,louizos2018l0} for differentiable discrete mask optimization.\n34\t\n35\t\\paragraph{MoE Routing and Gated Sparsity.}\n36\tExpert Choice routing~\\cite{zhou2022expertchoice} lets each expert select its top-$k$ tokens under a fixed capacity budget, a global allocation perspective that parallels TEAL's neuron-level global top-$k$.\n37\tD2D-MoE~\\cite{szatkowski2024d2dmoe} converts dense SwiGLU layers into dynamic-$k$ MoE by exploiting the same activation sparsity we target, but restructures the network into discrete experts rather than predicting masks on the original architecture.\n38\tGated architectures beyond SwiGLU (e.g., Griffin's RG-LRU~\\cite{de2024griffin}) produce analogous structured sparsity through their gating mechanisms, suggesting that output-distribution prediction may generalize beyond standard Transformer FFNs.\n39\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_018N6LYasBXquWk11d4UTTdE","session_id":"20b0e106-df0c-43c4-9dfa-fa82e00c09d1","uuid":"ef4ecdf2-a9ba-4cc6-ab64-f113d8b5f396","timestamp":"2026-05-18T18:31:18.431Z"}
